docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:55:> If you are asking "am I eligible for weight loss surgery?", this page explains the clinical criteria used in assessment, including BMI, weight-related health conditions, previous weight loss attempts, and what happens when you meet the Obesity Surgery WA team.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:145:> A surgical eligibility assessment gives you a personalised answer based on BMI, weight-related health conditions, previous weight loss attempts, medical risk, readiness for aftercare, and your goals.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:153:> At your first appointment, your surgeon reviews your weight history, health conditions, goals, and questions, then explains which pathways may be suitable.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:230:> Draft L84: As a general guide, surgical assessment is typically available for those with a BMI of ~35, and almost always from 40 and higher. Someone with a BMI of 30 to 34 may qualify where weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present and there have been previous attempts at weight loss through diet, exercise, or non-surgical means without lasting success. Please note that these are general clinical indicators only; your surgeon will make a clinical assessment and discuss your options with you when you meet.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:238:> Draft L92-L95: BMI 30-34 with a weight-related health condition: surgery may be appropriate at this range when conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present, which can strengthen the case for a surgical pathway.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:240:> BMI 30–34 with a weight-related health condition: this range does not meet the MBS threshold for these items. Your surgeon can assess your broader clinical picture and discuss non-MBS options where appropriate; a condition below BMI 35 does not by itself establish eligibility for MBS-funded surgery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:246:> Draft L97-L100: BMI 35-39: most people in this range are eligible for a surgical assessment, with or without other health conditions. A range of procedures may be suitable, and your surgeon will discuss the options most appropriate for your situation.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:262:> Draft L107-L111: Weight-related health conditions or comorbidities: certain conditions strengthen a surgical case regardless of BMI. These include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH), and severe acid reflux.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:264:> Weight-related health conditions: a major obesity-related comorbidity is relevant to MBS assessment at BMI 35–39.9, but a condition does not create MBS eligibility below BMI 35. Whether a particular condition counts as a major obesity-related comorbidity is assessed individually against the current MBS criteria; your surgeon will assess the full clinical picture.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:271:> Not necessarily. A BMI of 30-34 may qualify for a surgical pathway when weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present. If surgery is not the right starting point, our medical team can offer meaningful support through medication and dietitian-led care. The only way to know where you stand is a proper assessment.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:297:  > At your first appointment, your surgeon reviews your weight history, health conditions, goals and questions, then explains which pathways may be suitable. Costs and next steps are discussed without pressure to proceed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:390:- Removes the brief’s “may qualify”, “with or without other health conditions”, “almost everyone eligible” and “most effective long-term option” directions.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/am-i-eligible-codex.md:75:> If you are asking "am I eligible for weight loss surgery?", this page explains the clinical criteria used in assessment, including BMI, weight-related health conditions, previous weight loss attempts, and what happens when you meet the Obesity Surgery WA team.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/am-i-eligible-codex.md:79:> "Someone with a BMI of 30 to 34 may qualify where weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present and there have been previous attempts at weight loss through diet, exercise, or non-surgical means without lasting success."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/am-i-eligible-codex.md:85:> Someone with a BMI of 30 to 34 may be considered for surgical assessment when weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present, especially after previous non-surgical weight loss attempts have not led to lasting improvement.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/am-i-eligible-codex.md:89:> "Not necessarily. A BMI of 30-34 may qualify for a surgical pathway when weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/am-i-eligible-codex.md:95:> Not necessarily. A BMI of 30-34 may be considered for surgical assessment when weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present, but your surgeon needs to assess your full health picture before recommending a pathway.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/am-i-eligible-codex.md:187:> A surgical eligibility assessment gives you a personalised answer based on BMI, weight-related health conditions, previous weight loss attempts, medical risk, readiness for aftercare, and your goals.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/am-i-eligible-codex.md:197:> At your first appointment, your surgeon reviews your weight history, health conditions, goals, and questions, then explains which pathways may be suitable.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:19:> A sleeve gastrectomy is generally considered suitable for adults with a BMI of 35 or higher, or a BMI of 30-35 with associated weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, or reflux.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:107:> **4. How is a gastric sleeve different from a gastric bypass?** A gastric sleeve reduces stomach size without rerouting the small intestine. A gastric bypass reduces stomach size and reroutes part of the small intestine, so the procedures can have different nutritional and weight-loss effects. Reflux history, BMI, other health conditions and surgeon assessment all contribute to the choice; neither procedure is right for everyone.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:111:> **6. I get heartburn already. Is a sleeve still right for me?** Pre-existing reflux is an important factor to raise with your surgeon because a sleeve can worsen symptoms in some patients. A bypass may be discussed as an alternative for some people with significant GORD, but the choice requires individual assessment of reflux, BMI, other health conditions and surgical risk.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:13:> Non-surgical medical weight management may be worth discussing from a BMI of 27 or above, particularly where weight-related health conditions are present. Medication suitability depends on the specific medicine, your medical history and current Australian prescribing guidance, while surgical Medicare eligibility is assessed separately.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:73:   > Medical weight management uses non-surgical tools such as GLP-1 medications and structured dietary programs to support weight loss and improve related health conditions.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:176:> You may be eligible for weight loss surgery if you have a BMI of 40 or higher, or 35 or higher with a weight-related health condition such as type 2 diabetes or high blood pressure, and where diet and exercise alone have not produced long-term results.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/weight-loss-surgery-perth.md:178:> A BMI of 27 or above, particularly where a weight-related health condition is present, is a reason to request an individual medical assessment for non-surgical options. Surgical Medicare eligibility must be assessed separately; current MBS criteria refer to BMI 40+, or BMI 35–39.9 with a major co-morbidity, with suitability assessed by your surgeon.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:95:> 1. Initial enquiry and GP referral: most patients start with a GP referral, which can help with Medicare and private health requirements; if you contact OSWA directly, the team can guide you on what is needed. 2. Consultation with your surgeon: your surgeon reviews your history, goals and health conditions, then explains which surgical or non-surgical pathways may be appropriate. 3. Wider team assessment: this may include physician, dietitian and exercise physiology input. 4. Pre-surgery preparation: you receive procedure-specific preparation advice, including any pre-operative diet. 5. Procedure day: surgery is usually laparoscopic, with hospital stay and recovery guidance tailored to the procedure. 6. Long-term aftercare: follow-up, nutrition monitoring and habit support continue after surgery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:195:> Most bariatric surgeries in Australia are performed as laparoscopic keyhole surgeries, meaning less pain post-operatively, smaller scars, and generally, a faster recovery than open surgery. No procedure is a ‘one-size-fits-all’; your surgeon will spend time understanding your weight loss goals and recommend a procedure based on those, any accompanying health conditions, and your lifestyle.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:279:> Bariatric surgery is generally considered suitable for adults with a BMI of 40 or higher, or in specific circumstances for those with a BMI of 35 or higher when an obesity-related health condition is also present. People with a BMI between 30 and 35 may be candidates in specific circumstances, particularly where type 2 diabetes is poorly controlled.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:322:  > 1. **Initial enquiry and GP referral:** most patients start with a GP referral, which can help with Medicare and private health requirements; if you contact OSWA directly, the team can guide you on what is needed. 2. **Consultation with your surgeon:** your surgeon reviews your history, goals and health conditions, then explains which surgical or non-surgical pathways may be appropriate. 3. **Wider team assessment:** this may include physician, dietitian and exercise physiology input. 4. **Pre-surgery preparation:** you receive procedure-specific preparation advice, including any pre-operative diet. 5. **Procedure day:** surgery is usually laparoscopic, with hospital stay and recovery guidance tailored to the procedure. 6. **Long-term aftercare:** follow-up, nutrition monitoring and habit support continue after surgery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:375:  > **1. How do I know which bariatric procedure is right for me?** The right procedure depends on your health history, weight loss goals, existing health conditions (like reflux or diabetes), and your lifestyle. There is no universal answer; what works well for one person may not be the best fit for another. This is exactly what your initial consultation is for. Your surgeon will review your circumstances thoroughly and recommend the procedure most likely to deliver safe, sustained results for you specifically.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:413:> The choice depends on your medical history, eating patterns, health conditions, goals and willingness to follow the required aftercare. Your surgeon should explain likely benefits, limits, risks and monitoring needs for each option; no procedure or outcome is guaranteed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-sleeve-perth-codex.md:15:Quoted draft sentence: "A sleeve gastrectomy is generally considered suitable for adults with a BMI of 35 or higher, or a BMI of 30–35 with associated weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, or reflux."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-sleeve-perth-codex.md:181:- **Blocking #2 (BMI 30–35 wording) — BRIEF-INHERITED.** The brief itself mandates this exact framing (`content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:88`: "BMI of 35 or above, or BMI 30–35 with weight-related health conditions"). The MBS-accuracy concern stands, but this is not writer error — the brief needs the same correction. Frame as "fix draft AND brief."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:10:  Quote: "A gastric bypass is generally considered suitable for adults with a BMI of 35 or higher, or a BMI of 30–35 with associated weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, or reflux."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:123:  Suggested rewrite: "Beyond weight loss alone, gastric bypass may support improvements in weight-related health conditions and day-to-day quality of life for some patients, although outcomes vary."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/gastric-bypass-perth-codex.md:170:- **Blocking #2 (BMI wording) — BRIEF-INHERITED.** `content-briefs/gastric-bypass-perth.md:93` mandates "BMI of 35 or above, or BMI 30 or above with related health conditions." MBS-accuracy concern stands; fix draft AND brief.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:21:> A gastric bypass is generally considered suitable for adults with a BMI of 35 or higher, or a BMI of 30-35 with associated weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, or reflux.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:57:> Gastric bypass surgery is one of the oldest, most well-known bariatric procedures in Australia, with the Roux-en-Y gastric bypass being the most common and often the preferred option for those managing associated health conditions such as type 2 diabetes or gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD) in addition to overweight or obesity.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/gastric-bypass-perth.md:478:> Beyond weight loss alone, gastric bypass may support improvements in weight-related health conditions and day-to-day quality of life for some patients, although outcomes vary.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/bariatric-surgery-perth-codex.md:85:Suggested rewrite: "1. Initial enquiry and GP referral: most patients start with a GP referral, which can help with Medicare and private health requirements; if you contact OSWA directly, the team can guide you on what is needed. 2. Consultation with your surgeon: your surgeon reviews your history, goals and health conditions, then explains which surgical or non-surgical pathways may be appropriate. 3. Wider team assessment: this may include physician, dietitian and exercise physiology input. 4. Pre-surgery preparation: you receive procedure-specific preparation advice, including any pre-operative diet. 5. Procedure day: surgery is usually laparoscopic, with hospital stay and recovery guidance tailored to the procedure. 6. Long-term aftercare: follow-up, nutrition monitoring and habit support continue after surgery."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:9:Suggested rewrite: "Non-surgical medical weight management may be worth discussing from a BMI of 27 or above, particularly where weight-related health conditions are present. Medication suitability depends on the specific medicine, your medical history and current Australian prescribing guidance, while surgical Medicare eligibility is assessed separately."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:51:Quote: "You may be eligible for weight loss surgery if you have a BMI of 40 or higher, or 35 or higher with a weight-related health condition such as type 2 diabetes or high blood pressure, and where diet and exercise alone have not produced long-term results."
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/_worklog/weight-loss-surgery-perth-codex.md:53:Suggested rewrite: "A BMI of 27 or above, particularly with a weight-related health condition, may be enough to start a conversation with our medical team about non-surgical options. Surgical options are usually discussed at higher BMI ranges; current MBS guidance for clinically severe obesity generally refers to BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with major co-morbidities, with suitability assessed by your surgeon."

